gs window](settings.md#the-mac-settings-window)); the launcher's C and Swift sources and the Swift compiler are in the app's reuse key. Cheat files go in `cheats/` in the Application Support folder ([cheats](cheats.md)). The launcher passes SIGTERM, SIGINT and SIGHUP on to the host and returns the host's exit code. When the bundle's game data is missing, it shows an alert.
+The launcher sets the host's environment from the bundle, as the iOS app's `main.m` does, and runs the host as a child process. The save goes to the game folder, the folder holding the `.app`, as `Saves/save` (`DM_SAVE`), and screenshots and recordings to its `Screenshots` and `Recordings` folders (`DM_GAME_FOLDER`), so everything of a game sits beside it. The first time, a save in `~/Library/Application Support/nxnative/<title ID>/save` is copied there (into a temporary folder beside it, then renamed into place); the old one is never moved or deleted, and when both hold a save the game folder's is used and neither is touched. Where the game folder is no place for them (not writable, an Applications folder, or the read-only copy macOS runs a quarantined app from), or with `DM_GAME_FOLDER=0` in an `env.txt`, the save stays in Application Support and captures go to `~/Pictures/NXForge` and `~/Movies/NXForge`. `pso_user.tsv` (`DM_PSO_USER`, with `pso_archive/` beside it) and temporary files go to `~/Library/Application Support/nxnative/<title ID>/` (`temp/`; that folder is also `DM_ROOT` and the working folder). The host's output goes to `~/Library/Logs/nxnative/<title ID>.log`, and the previous run's is kept as `<title ID>.prev.log`. Environment switches go in `env.txt` in the Application Support folder, then in the bundle's `dm/env.txt` (one `KEY=VALUE` per line). Neither replaces a variable that is already set. Then come the player's saved choices, `launcher.<VAR>` in the app's preferences domain, each checked against `DMSettings` (the values `launcher.json` offers) ([Saved choices](frontends.md#saved-choices)); `DM_SAVED_CHOICES=0` ignores them. Before that, unless `DM_LAUNCHER=0` says otherwise, the launcher runs the cloud save check in its own process (`dm/mac/nxforge/launcher_save_check.swift`, upstream's `SaveCheck`); NXForge's apps show no settings window, since NXForge.app writes the saved choices ([The Mac settings window](settings.md#the-mac-settings-window)); the launcher's C and Swift sources and the Swift compiler are in the app's reuse key. Cheat files go in `cheats/` in the Application Support folder ([cheats](cheats.md)). The launcher passes SIGTERM, SIGINT and SIGHUP on to the host and returns the host's exit code. When the bundle's game data is missing, it shows an alert.
 
 Why the host is a child process: macOS privacy checks (TCC) cover files on removable volumes. A launcher that hands its process over to the host, such as a shell script that `exec`s `dmhost`, got EPERM on every open on the external drive, with no prompt. That happened with ad-hoc and Developer ID signatures alike, and after `tccutil reset`. A compiled launcher as `CFBundleExecutable` that `posix_spawn`s `dmhost`, which sits beside it in `Contents/MacOS`, can read those files. An app that sits on the game drive needs this, and so does an env file that points at files there.
 
